SPU: an interpreter handoff stops at the innermost open call bracket's return address (hop_ret_pc) for every image.

Port of patch 7063 (spu-interp-stop-at-open-bracket-return, branch rc-sse41 b3b73f5, never on main), regenerated
2026-11-24 with exact context against the fully patched tree (every port/patches/*.patch up to 7115) and renumbered after
7115: in its old slot it broke 7099 (spu_channels.c, the resolver interpreter block) and 7106, and after the series it no
longer applied without fuzz. Changes against 7063: the resolver-path hunk sits around 7099's frame checks (the stop and
the owned-stop note come before 7099's site-5 check, so a stop at an OLDER framed call's return reached from deeper still
unwinds there), and the log tag reads "(R&C 7120)". Behaviour and host test are 7063's.

Games: Tools of Destruction (PhysColl, job module 5c9062ea153f_eDFF8, overlay 207, under policy module pm_10053d00;
dev levels 12 "zordoom prison" and 17 "meridian city" through the game's -level argv). Shared runtime code: every title
that runs unlifted SPU code under a lifted call.

Bug: a host frame leaked per call until the depth guard (KZ3 patch 1086, SPU_HOST_DEPTH_MAX 2000) halted the SPU, and
PhysColl never completed (the game stalls for good: the PPU main thread waits in 0x5FB2F0 for PhysColl, prints
"For PhysColl, WwsJobDelay timed out", and no frame is drawn again). The lifted job loops through `bisl $0,$91` at LS
0xA3FC (link 0xA400) into a plugin at 0x1B360 that has no lift (lvl12: `bisl $0` at 0x7870, link 0x7998, into 0x1D930;
a second site 0xCCAC -> 0x1BE00; the plugins sit above the module's 0x4000..0x121A0 image). The generated bracket
publishes hop_ret_pc = the link, host_depth++, and calls spu_indirect_branch at once; its KZ3 1069 interpreter fallback
(spu_channels.c) passed stop 0 for an image >= 0 (only the image -1 case got hop_ret_pc, R&C patch 7039). The plugin's
`bi $0` to the link therefore did not stop: the link is mid-function (no lifted entry), so the interpreter ran the
caller's continuation inside the callee's host frame and rejoined lifted code at the next entry (0xA410; lvl12 0x7808 or 0x7870).
spu_drain_call(link) then saw a pc != its return pc and ran the caller's loop as callee work, one host level deeper; the
next pass's bisl nested again. Evidence (N = interpreter entry, J = where it stopped, /d = host depth mod 256):
- work/proton/tods-play-1114 (2026-10-22): [spu-depth] img=2 host_depth=2000; ring `0A3B4 N:1B360/205 J:0A410/205`,
  then /206, /207: one level per loop pass, and J is 0xA410, never the bracket's 0xA400.
- ls-tods-lvl12-zordoom-prison-1123-231208 (2026-11-23, main exe, -level "zordoom prison"): last flip 5030 at t=113 s,
  then `[spu-depth] img=2 pc=0x07808 lr=0x07998 host_depth=2000`, ring `N:1D930/199 J:07808/199` .. `/208`, and four
  "For PhysColl, WwsJobDelay timed out" lines (app_stdout.txt:6196-6242). Lifted call site:
  work/spu/tods/lift/jobmod_5c9062ea153f_eDFF8/spu_recomp.c:4382 (hop_ret_pc = 0x7998; host_depth++;
  spu_indirect_branch; spu_drain_call(ctx, 0x7998)).
- lvl17-meridian-city-1113-121244 (2026-11-13): two PhysColl lanes halt at depth 2000 with rings N:1BE00 -> J:0DA34 and
  N:1B360 -> J:0A410 (app_stdout.txt:5619-5634).
The microstep -> interpreter handoff (spu_smc_microstep_body) had the same gap from the other side: it stopped at
drain_ret_pc, which an immediate bisl has not set yet (it still names the outer bracket, or 0 after the inline fast drain).

Fix: both handoffs stop at hop_ret_pc, the innermost open bracket's return address (published before the callee runs,
kept by spu_drain_call_full, restored when the bracket closes, reset at run start, stack reset and IRQ landing). Stopped
there, the fallback leaves spu_indirect_branch pending with pc == the bracket's return, which spu_drain_call_body's first
test consumes (or, from the microstep, nothing pending and the inline drain's pc test): the continuation runs once, lifted,
in the host frame that owns it. This is the rule KZ3 patch 1097 already applies to a lifted computed hop (pc ==
hop_ret_pc returns to the bracket) and R&C 7039 to image -1 contexts. Image -1 behaviour is unchanged.
RC_SPU_OWNED_STOP=0 restores the old stops (A/B only; default on: a pure fix). "[spu] owned-stop (R&C 7120)" logs the
first 8 stops the old rule would have missed (any site: ToD makes 16+ img=2 resolver handoffs before PhysColl in every
-level run, so the PhysColl site may not be among the 8; name the site with SPU_INTERP_HIST=1, not with this note).
Not a token-based return contract (docs/in-progress/spu-return-contract-2026-11-07.md): the stop is a PC test against
the bracket that is provably innermost; an interpreted recursive call through the same call site would still stop early
(no such case in a lifted caller, whose entry the interpreter rejoins lifted). Not a cap: SPU_HOST_DEPTH_MAX is unchanged.
If a run with this patch shows no [spu-depth] but still stalls with WwsJobDelay, that is a second cause (the 0x7870 loop
walks a linked list: a list that loops back on itself), not a failure of this fix.

Host test: tools/test_spu_interpreter_handoff.py (runtime/spu/tests/test_spu_interpreter_handoff.c): nested_entry now
publishes hop_ret_pc like the generated bracket; new modes nested-return-immediate (0x204 registered),
nested-return-immediate-unlifted (the PhysColl shape: 0x204 unlifted, must not run interpreted, bracket depth back to 0)
and nested-return-immediate-unlifted-microstep (policy_mode 0, the microstep handoff), each unlifted mode with an A/B
control: with RC_SPU_OWNED_STOP=0 it must fail. The .py checks the owned-stop note is logged for the unlifted returns
and not for 0x204 registered (rc_spu_interp_rejoins_at in spu_interp.c skips stops the old rule made anyway).
The test builds against the patched tree: g_spu_trampoline_fn is rebound to the file-scope ctx (KZ3 1017), the .py links
runtime/diag/kz3_wait_acct.c (KZ3 1085/1135), the stop modes expect the STOPPED_BY_STOP status of patch 1069's
policy-mode interpreter path, and the stepcap modes run with policy_mode 0. Run from the patched tree:
python3 tools/test_spu_interpreter_handoff.py (R&C wrapper: port/rc/tests/run_spu_interp_handoff_test.sh, in
tools/host-tests.txt).

